{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli Summary**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. In 1164 Raymond III was captured at the Battle of Harim by Nur ad-Din and held prisoner until 1172. During his captivity Tripoli was ruled by his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch and his mother Hodierna.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nAfter his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and resumed his rule. He was a key figure in the politics of the Crusader states during the 12th century. Raymond III played a major role in the conflicts between the Crusader states and the Muslim powers. He was involved in several battles and alliances including the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where he fought alongside Baldwin IV of Jerusalem against Saladin. Raymond III also negotiated truces and marriages to maintain peace and alliances among the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III was also known for his diplomatic skills and his efforts to unite the Crusader states against the growing threat of Saladin. He was a strong advocate for cooperation between the Crusader states and often mediated disputes between them. In 1185 Raymond III became regent for Baldwin V of Jerusalem the young king who died shortly after. Raymond III then supported Sibylla of Jerusalem and Guy of Lusignan as rulers of Jerusalem but their rule led to the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where Saladin captured Raymond III and most of the Crusader army.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly after his release from captivity. He was succeeded by his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch who became Bohemond IV of Tripoli. Raymond IIIs death 